    "body": "I agree with your intuition of how structs work. We probably need to expand on object identity and reference equality as we do also make those kind of comparisons, e.g., between nodes. I also think you want to compare structs using reference equality at times. Say you iterate over a list of them and want a condition to vary based on a pre-selected struct.\n\nI think \"unordered\" needs to be explicit as it's a mismatch with the type. I would be okay with \"ordered\" also being explicit, but I also think it's fine if we don't label that \"ordered\". (Structs are not ordered however so they don't need the qualifier.)\n\nThough here again it would make sense if you could define that for your set \"equals\" means \"unordered deep equals\" so callers don't have to know the particulars of your set and can always rely on \"equals\" (that will also make recursion work better where I suppose we branch on primitive/non-primitive and then use the default \"equals\").\n\n---\n\nSo primitive types don't have \"equals\", they only have \"is\". Non-primitive types have \"equals\" (possibly plus \"deep\" and \"unordered\" qualifiers), but they also have \"is\" for identity comparison. That seems quite nice.",
